<h2>What the Combine Cannot Measure</h2>
<p>The combine measures eight physical attributes. None of them is basketball IQ. None is processing speed under pressure. None is footwork precision, release quickness, or the ability to make the right decision in half a second with a 7'3" defender's hand in your face.</p>
<h3>The Game 4 Comeback — What Physical Tests Miss</h3>
<p>Down 29 in the third quarter at Madison Square Garden — the largest deficit in Finals history — Brunson scored 36 points on 12-of-25 shooting with 7 assists and 3 steals. The Knicks outscored San Antonio 49-19 over the final 17 minutes. None of that is measurable at a combine. The calm required to run a precise half-court offense down 20 with five minutes left. The footwork to create a pull-up jumper against a 12-foot-8-inch peak reach when a single turnover ends the game. The decision speed to find the right pass when every defender collapses. These are real and decisive athletic qualities that the combine has never attempted to measure.</p>
<h3>Change of Speed</h3>
<p>Brunson's most dangerous tool is the ability to change speed within two dribbles — slow to 30 percent, then explode to 100. Against Wembanyama, who hedges on pick-and-rolls but cannot fully close out laterally, this creates the half-second windows his pull-up jumper requires. His elite 10.59 lane agility score is the one combine number that hints at this quality — but even that is a proxy, not the thing itself.</p>
<h3>Foul Drawing</h3>
<p>Brunson draws fouls at one of the highest rates in the league relative to shot attempts. Against Wembanyama, whose extraordinary length creates incidental contact on every close contest, free throw generation is one of the two or three most reliable paths to scoring. Brunson shot 9-of-11 from the line in Game 4. No combine test predicts that.</p>
